.TH XL-SCREENSHOT 1 "November 2026" "xl-Screenshot 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xl-screenshot \- take a screenshot of the screen, a window, or a selection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xl-screenshot
[\-\-selection] [\-\-tray] [\-\-version]
.br
.B xl-screenshot
[\fIWHAT TO CAPTURE\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xl-Screenshot
captures the whole desktop, a single monitor (with more than one, the one the pointer is on),
a single window (with its titlebar, optionally with a shadow), or an area drawn over a still
picture of the desktop. It uses Cinnamon's own screenshot service when there is one, which
also makes it work on Cinnamon's Wayland session; otherwise it captures through X11.
A delay of up to 11 seconds can be set before the capture; on X11, Esc cancels it. The capture can be
copied, saved as PNG, JPEG, or WebP, or marked up in
.BR xl-annotate (1).
.PP
Only one copy runs at a time: starting it again brings up the window that is already open.
.PP
With "Accurate Selection" (the default) chosen in the menu, a Selection can be adjusted
with its handles before it is captured; with "Fast Selection", it is captured as soon as
the mouse button is let go.
.PP
With "Show in System Tray" turned on in its menu, clicking the tray icon starts a Selection
capture, closing the window only hides it, and xl-Screenshot starts at login.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-selection
Start a Selection capture right away. If xl-Screenshot is already running, the running copy
starts it. Useful for a keyboard shortcut.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tray
Start with just the tray icon (what the login entry uses).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version and quit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show all the options and quit.
.SH COMMAND-LINE CAPTURES
With any of the options below,
.B xl-screenshot
captures, saves and exits, with no window and no tray icon, whether or not the app is
running. That suits scripts and keyboard shortcuts. Many options follow
.BR scrot (1),
and scrot's spellings work too. Positions and sizes are in real pixels, even on a HiDPI
desktop. The path of the saved file is printed. The exit status is 0 when the image was
saved, 1 when nothing was captured (canceled, or the capture failed), and 2 for a mistake
in the options.
.SS What to capture (one of)
.TP
.BR \-\-screens ", " \-m ", " \-\-multidisp
Every screen, as the Screens button. The default.
.TP
.BR \-\-screen " [\fIN\fR], " \-M ", " \-\-monitor
Screen \fIN\fR, numbered as \fB\-\-list\-screens\fR shows; without \fIN\fR, the one the
pointer is on (X11 only: on Wayland, apps aren't told where the pointer is).
.TP
.BR \-\-area " \fIX,Y,W,H\fR, " \-a ", " \-\-autoselect
A rectangle: its top-left corner at \fIX,Y\fR, \fIW\fR by \fIH\fR pixels.
.TP
.BR \-\-selection ", " \-s ", " \-\-select
Draw the area, as with the Selection button (Accurate or Fast, as chosen in the app).
.TP
.B \-\-window
Pick a window, as with the Window button.
.TP
.BR \-\-focused ", " \-u ", " \-\-focussed
The window that has the focus.
.TP
.BR \-\-window\-id " \fIWID\fR, " \-w
A window by its X11 id, as
.BR xwininfo (1)
shows it. X11 only.
.SS Where it goes
.TP
.BR \-\-file " \fIFILE\fR, " \-F ", or just " \fIFILE\fR
Save here. \fB\-\fR writes the image to standard output. The name can hold
.BR strftime (3)
codes such as %Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S, and $w and $h for the image's width and height ($$ for a $).
Missing folders are made. Without a file, captures go to Pictures/xl-Screenshot.
.TP
.BR \-\-overwrite ", " \-o
Replace a file that is already there. Otherwise _1, _2... is added to the name.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FMT"
png, jpeg (or jpg) or webp. Otherwise it comes from the file name's extension, or is PNG.
.TP
.BR \-\-quality " \fI1-100\fR, " \-q
JPEG and WebP quality; 90 if not given.
.TP
.B \-\-clipboard
Copy the image. It can still be pasted after xl-screenshot has exited. On its own, nothing is
saved; with a file, both.
.TP
.B \-\-annotate
Open the capture in
.BR xl-annotate (1).
.SS Options
.TP
.BR \-\-delay " \fISEC\fR, " \-d
Wait this many seconds first. On X11, Esc cancels.
.TP
.BR \-\-pointer ", " \-p
Include the pointer.
.TP
.B \-\-shadow
Window captures: include the window's shadow.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-screens
List the screens, with their numbers, sizes and positions, and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B xl-screenshot \-\-area 100,100,400,300 \-\-file ~/area.png
A rectangle, saved as area.png.
.TP
.B xl-screenshot \-\-selection \-\-clipboard
Draw an area and copy it: a handy keyboard shortcut.
.TP
.B xl-screenshot \-\-screen 2 \-d 3 'Screen-2_%H%M%S.jpg'
Screen 2 after three seconds, as a JPEG named with the time.
.TP
.B xl-screenshot \-a 0,0,800,600 \- | magick \- \-resize 50% small.png
Straight into ImageMagick.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/xl-screenshot/config.json
The save format, delay, last save folder, and other settings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/io.github.xlcheese.XlScreenshot.desktop
Starts xl-Screenshot at login; present only while the tray icon is turned on.
.SH AUTHOR
Wesley Hayslip.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xl-annotate (1),
.BR scrot (1),
https://github.com/xlcheese/xl-screenshot
